{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Farners and ruled as Count of Tripoli from 1149 to 1177 he was also a Prince of Galilee and a regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem Raymond III was born in 1142 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helena of Armenia\n\n* **Early Life** Raymond III succeeded his father at the age of seven after Raymond II was assassinated in 1149 his mother Helena acted as regent until he came of age in 1156\n\nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a close ally of King Amalric I he supported Amalric in his campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate in Egypt and participated in the Siege of Damietta in 1169\n\n* **Regency and Military Campaigns** In 1174 Raymond III became regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem for the young King Henry I he worked to strengthen the kingdoms defenses and formed alliances with the Byzantine Empire and the County of Tripoli\n\nRaymond III was known for his military prowess and led several successful campaigns against the forces of Nur ad-Din Zangi he also defended the city of Tripoli against a siege by Nur ad-Dins forces in 1174\n\n* **Later Life and Death** Raymond III died in 1177 without an heir his death led to a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli which was eventually resolved with the accession of William of Burgundy Raymond IIIs legacy was that of a skilled military leader and a key figure in the politics of the Crusader states \n\nHe was buried in the Church of Saint Julian in Tripoli his death marked the end of the Farners dynasty in Tripoli and led to a period of instability in the region \n\nRaymond IIIs life and reign are well documented in historical records including the works of medieval historians such as William of Tyre and Ibn al-Athir \n\nHis role as regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem helped to stabilize the region during a period of turmoil and his military campaigns helped to secure key victories for the Crusader states \n\nDespite his successes Raymond IIIs death without an heir led to a period of uncertainty in Tripoli and the surrounding regions \n\nThe County of Tripoli continued to play a key role in the Crusader states after Raymond IIIs death but it faced significant challenges including attacks from Muslim forces and internal power struggles \n\nRaymond IIIs legacy as a military leader and statesman has endured and he remains an important figure in the history of the Crusades \n\nHis life and reign provide valuable insights into the politics and military conflicts of the medieval Middle East \n\nRaymond IIIs relationships with other Crusader leaders such as King Amalric I were critical to his success and helped to shape the course of the Crusades \n\nOverall Raymond IIIs reign was marked by significant military and political achievements despite the challenges he faced as a ruler in a volatile region \n\nHis death in 1177 marked the end of an era in Tripoli and had far-reaching consequences for the Crusader states \n\nRaymond IIIs story is a testament to the complex and often tumultuous nature of medieval politics in the Middle East \n\nIt highlights the importance of alliances and military strength in securing power and territory during this period \n\nRaymond IIIs life and legacy continue to be studied by historians today providing a unique window into the history of the Crusades and the medieval Middle East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.2819894552230835}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.288792165927589e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -8.093983342405409e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.021452646702528}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -6.294052582234144e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.597391747054644e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.0517112463712692e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.021082237362861633}, {"token": " a", "logpr